/* ==============================================================
   07 — MoveJump: the SCARA's own move
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   MoveJump, SetMoveJumpHeight, SetMoveJumpApproachVel
   NEEDS     Robot activated, no tool mounted
   WATCH     The same hop, written three moves long and then one
   TIME      ~35 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE SHAPE A SCARA LIVES IN

   Almost everything a SCARA does in production has the same
   shape: lift straight up off a part, travel across, come
   straight down onto the next spot. Written with the commands so
   far, that is three moves:

       MoveLin(<above here>)       straight up
       MovePose(<above there>)     across
       MoveLin(<there>)            straight down

   Three moves means two forced stops — remember that a MoveLin
   and a MovePose never blend — and three commands' worth of
   planning. The MCS500 has one command that does the whole hop:

       MoveJump(x, y, z, γ)

   Same four arguments as MovePose: the pose where you want to
   LAND. The robot lifts straight up from where it is, travels
   across, and comes straight down onto the target. The manual
   says it is highly optimised for pick and place, and much
   faster than the three-move version. Watch the two versions
   below and you will not need the stopwatch.

   THE TWO SETTINGS THAT SHAPE THE HOP

       SetMoveJumpHeight(hstart, hend, hmin, hmax)

     hstart   how far to lift straight up before going across
     hend     how far above the target to come straight down from
     hmin     the lowest the hop may peak, above the higher of the
              start and the end
     hmax     the highest it may peak, likewise

   All in mm. Default: 10, 10, 0, 102 — lift and land through
   10 mm of straight vertical, and let the robot choose the
   fastest hop in between. Raise hmin to clear an obstacle
   between the two points; lower hmax to keep the hop under
   something overhead. Constraining either can cost cycle time,
   because it takes away the robot's freedom to pick the fastest
   path. (The heights are measured along the base's z, so they
   are positive for a robot standing on a table.)

       SetMoveJumpApproachVel(vstart, pstart, vend, pend)

     vstart, pstart   cap the vertical speed at vstart mm/s for
                      the first pstart mm of the lift
     vend, pend       cap it at vend mm/s for the last pend mm of
                      the landing

   Default: 10, 1, 10, 1 — the first and last millimetre at
   10 mm/s. A gentle touch built in, on every jump. Lengthen pend
   to land a delicate part softly; set a speed to 0 for no cap
   at all.

   WHAT IT IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT

   MoveJump is a JOINT-SPACE move, like MovePose:

     - its speed comes from SetJointVel and SetJointAcc, not
       SetCartLinVel
     - the posture and turn at the landing obey SetConf and
       SetConfTurn, or the fastest choice if those are automatic
     - the travel in the middle is a curve, not a straight line,
       and its highest point can be anywhere along it — not
       necessarily halfway

   And one rule from geometry: the lift and the hop happen ABOVE
   the start and the end, so a jump needs room up there. A start
   or a landing with the shaft near the top of its 102 mm stroke
   has nowhere to lift to. Keep pick and place heights well down
   the stroke — the zero position, shaft fully up, is the worst
   possible place to jump from.

   Two rules from the manual, both easy to break:

     - BLENDING OFF. Two MoveJumps in a row, or a MoveJump
       followed by a MovePose, need SetBlending(0) first, or the
       jump will not be completed as planned.
     - NOT IN TIME-BASED MODE. MoveJump must not be used after
       SetMoveMode(1). See 03_advanced/06.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(30)
SetCartLinVel(150)

// Blending off for the whole file: MoveJump needs it, and the
// three-move version stops at every corner anyway.
SetBlending(0)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)

// Start down at "the pick", 40 mm above the bench.
MovePose(170, -70, 40, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- The long way: three moves ---------------------------------
   Up, across, down — then back the same way. Note the dead stop
   at each top corner. */
MoveLin(170, -70, 80, 0)
MovePose(170, 70, 80, 0)
MoveLin(170, 70, 40, 0)
Delay(1)

MoveLin(170, 70, 80, 0)
MovePose(170, -70, 80, 0)
MoveLin(170, -70, 40, 0)
Delay(1.5)


/* --- The SCARA way: one move -----------------------------------
   The same hop, there and back, one line each way. No stops at
   the corners, and the robot picks the height of the hop. Watch
   the last millimetre of each landing slow right down — that is
   the default approach speed. */
MoveJump(170, 70, 40, 0)
Delay(1)
MoveJump(170, -70, 40, 0)
Delay(1.5)


/* --- Clearing something in between -----------------------------
   Imagine a fixture wall standing between the two points. Lift
   and land through 20 mm of straight vertical, and make the hop
   peak between 40 and 60 mm above the start — clear of the wall,
   but no higher than needed. */
SetMoveJumpHeight(20, 20, 40, 60)

MoveJump(170, 70, 40, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Landing softly --------------------------------------------
   A delicate part: cap the last 10 mm of the landing at
   20 mm/s. The lift is untouched. */
SetMoveJumpApproachVel(10, 1, 20, 10)

MoveJump(170, -70, 40, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Turning the part on the way -------------------------------
   γ changes too: the part is turned 90 degrees during the hop,
   for free. Joint 4 does it while the arm travels. */
MoveJump(170, 70, 40, 90)
Delay(1)
MoveJump(170, -70, 40, 0)
Delay(1)


// --- Defaults back, and home ----------------------------------
SetMoveJumpHeight(10, 10, 0, 102)
SetMoveJumpApproachVel(10, 1, 10, 1)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
SetBlending(100)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Run the three-move block and the MoveJump block back to
      back. Same two points, same SetJointVel. This is the demo
      that explains why SCARAs exist.

   2. Raise SetJointVel to 100 and run the MoveJump block alone.
      Then ask what their current pick-and-place cycle time is.

   3. In the "landing softly" block, change pend from 10 to 30.
      That is longer than the 20 mm vertical landing, so the
      whole landing now crawls in at 20 mm/s. It is the dial for
      fragile parts, and its cost in cycle time is plain to see.

   NEXT   03_advanced/01_variables.mxprog — programs that are not
          hard-coded.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
